  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nAnother round of rainfall occurred early this morning, albeit \nsignificantly lighter than what occurred on Friday. Most locations \nsaw anywhere from an additional 0.25-0.50\" with the latter end of \nthat range more localized to the mountains and foothills. While it's \nstill humid and cloudy out there this morning with a few measly \nshowers near the coastline, drier air will be filtering into the \narea through the afternoon as weak upper level ridging builds in \nbehind the previous system. Slightly warmer temperatures can be \nexpected on Sunday, just about or slightly below normal, with partly \ncloudy skies as we enjoy our break in between weather systems. \nSunday might be the warmest day of the week as multiple rounds of \nlarger upper level troughs/lows will advance through CA next week, \nbringing multiple chances for rain and/or winds, along with \ncooler/well-below-normal temperatures. \n\nThe first system of note is going to be a elongated trough \nstretching from Canada down into Baja, poised to approach the \nCalifornia Bight Monday afternoon. As it approaches, increasing \ninstability over the waters and near the coastline could produce \nsome isolated thunderstorms late Monday/early Tuesday, in a very \nsimilar fashion to what occurred Friday morning. Should these \nthunderstorms develop and be able to move ashore, intermittent heavy \nrainfall and stronger winds will be possible, most likely pre-\nsunrise Tuesday morning. CAPE values, while mostly unimpressive, do \nindicate hot spots over the marine waters and into Orange County/IE \nduring this time frame. Soundings indicate that any storms will move \nquickly, so no widespread flooding is expected. Outside of the \nthunderstorm threat, some area showers will move across as the open \ntrough moves inland Tuesday morning, with less widespread rainfall \nas experienced the past two days. Instead of a closed low like the \nFriday storms, this system will remain an open trough and thus is \nexpected to have less moisture associated with it. While \nprecipitation amounts have been trending down, snow levels \nassociated with this colder system are expected to hover right \naround 6000ft MSL Tue morning, allowing for some light accumulations \nat only the highest elevations. Because of the lighter QPF, the \npattern does not currently support more than a trace/localized inch \nor so of snow. \n\nAny and all precipitation with this next system should move out \nTuesday afternoon, with another dry spell Wednesday with zonal flow \naloft. Temperatures won't have much time to rebound before the \nfollowing system arrives, and high temperatures continue to slowly \nfall through the week, bottoming out at 8-15 degrees below normal. \nGlobal models have better agreement on the next system today than \nthey have the past several runs, indicating a deepening low will \nmove down along the CA coastline before trekking eastward across \nCentral CA late Thursday. Should this play pan out, the system may \nbring more cold and windy conditions for SoCal with less rainfall \nthan previously expected. Still, much can change between now and \nThursday, with a slide further south providing more ample moisture \nstill a possibility late next week. Once better confidence is had on \nthis low, a more detailed picture can be provided. For now, have \nmaintained at least some rainfall in the Thursday-Friday time frame, \nas well as light accumulating snow for the higher terrain of the San \nBernardino Mountains. \n\nAdditional mention and details of impacts with these systems next \nweek are discussed below in the beaches and marine discussions. \nPlease read these below.",
